From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Name the accounts behind per-user SIDs

Multi-user debt D12. In a prefix shared between Unix users, each user has the
SID S-1-5-21-0-0-0-<1000 + uid> (patch 0002), but LookupAccountSid knew only
RID 1000, "the first user", so every other user's SID had no name:
PowerShell's [Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent().Name was
empty, and installers, logs and ACL editors showed nothing. LookupAccountName
likewise found only the calling user.

The server assigned those SIDs, so it answers: a new sg_lookup_account request
maps a RID to its Unix account name, or a name to its RID, from the passwd
database (a Windows name is matched as given, then in lower case). The
prefix's owner and root are SYSTEM, which keeps its well-known name, and are
not answered. advapi32 asks it for local-computer SIDs with a RID above 1000
that it does not already know, and for account names it does not otherwise
resolve; the domain is the computer name and the use SidTypeUser, as for the
calling user. Outside a shared prefix the server answers NOT_SUPPORTED and
nothing changes.

Tested: as a second Unix user on a shared server, the user's own SID resolves
to COMPUTER\sgconf, the name sgconf (and SGCONF) to that SID and back;
unknown names, root and group names are not mapped. advapi32:security and
advapi32:lsa pass unchanged in an ordinary prefix.

